<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Longhouse Religion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Code of Handsome Lake, Gaiwiio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Longhouse Religion is a Haudenosaunee faith founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handsome-lake">Handsome Lake</a> after his visions of 1799. It combines the traditional cycle of thanksgiving ceremonies with a moral code, the Gaiwiio, that condemned alcohol and witchcraft and stressed family life. It is still practised in longhouses across Iroquois communities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/longhouse-religion.jpg" alt="Longhouse Religion" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arthur C. Parker (Life time: (1881 – 1955)) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Onondaga_Longhouse.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handsome-lake">Handsome Lake</a>, Sganyodaiyoʼ in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, received his revelations in a trance at the age of 64, after a lifetime of poverty and alcoholism, and then stopped drinking and began to preach. His teaching came in the wake of war, land loss, dispossession and social disruption among the Seneca and other Haudenosaunee peoples. Its message, known in the Haudenosaunee languages by names meaning Good Message, called for moral reform and the restoration of right living. Encoded in wampum, it spread through western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and Haudenosaunee country. The movement is named for the longhouses, once large extended-family homes, which it keeps for its ceremonies.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handsome-lake">Handsome Lake</a> entrusted the preaching to holders of the message, six of them in 1912. Because the teaching passed by word of mouth, versions diverged. In the 1860s the holders met at Cold Spring, Handsome Lake's former home, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> chief John Jacket settled the correct text and wrote it down in Seneca; the group then memorised it at Cattaraugus. That copy was later lost, and in 1903 Chief Edward Cornplanter rewrote it from memory for the New York State Archives; William Bluesky, a Baptist lay preacher, translated it into English.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Good Message is proclaimed twice a year, at the Midwinter Thanksgiving between 15 January and 15 February and at the Six Nations meeting in September, often by preachers exchanged between reservations. A full recitation takes three mornings. Before sunrise each day the preacher stands before the longhouse fireplace and sings the Sun Song for good weather, with an assistant beside him holding a white wampum strand. The atmosphere can resemble a revival meeting, and many participants publicly declare their allegiance again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>First called the new religion in contrast to older practice, it has come to be known as the Old Way in contrast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The anthropologist Anthony F. C. Wallace counted about 5,000 members in 1969. Some Haudenosaunee traditionalists reject the Code as too influenced by Christianity and follow instead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-law-of-peace">Great Law of Peace</a> of Deganawidah, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">Great Peacemaker</a>. Handsome Lake's revelations came in the Burned-over district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> months before the revivals of the Second Great Awakening, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-smith">Joseph Smith</a>, founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mormonism">Mormonism</a>, is believed to have been influenced by them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Longhouse Religion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late 18th century the Seneca chief Handsome Lake founded a new religion joining Quaker beliefs to Iroquoian tradition, the Gaihwi꞉io or <b>Longhouse Religion</b>; by the 1960s at least 50% of Haudenosaunee followed it.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 18th century the war chief Cornplanter and the orator Red Jacket led the nation, and in 1799 Handsome Lake began the teaching that became the <b>Longhouse religion</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/seneca-nation">Seneca Nation</a></li></ul></section>
